		(uuid "eb2feb28-f7a6-44de-a558-693b14144d50")
	)
	(text
		"NOTES\n1. Budget (brick 15 A, keep under 12 A): ~1.5 A drivers/gateway + ~0.2 A per moving module (9 A with all 45 moving).\n    The gateway gives the LEDs what is left: cap = 10.5 A - 0.2 A x modules moving, at most ~7 A (full white).\n    The strip runs straight off +12V (no fuse). Row fuses 7 A hold vs ~3.2 A worst case per row (15 x ~0.2 A).\n2. D1 (SMCJ15A) is a crowbar: if J1 is wired backwards it conducts and the brick's short-circuit protection shuts it down.\n3. Main switch: a 15 A rocker wired to J10 with 16 AWG carries the full load (~11 A worst case). Use one rated for 12 V DC.\n    Check the brick plug with a meter before first power-up: J1 expects jack pins 2+4 = +12 V, 1+3 = GND (Kycon numbers).\n    If it is reversed: cut NT1 and NT2, then wire the NT holes: jack pins 1+3 (NT2.1) -> +12V (NT1.2), pins 2+4 (NT1.1) -> GND (NT2.2).\n4. The LED strip has no hardware switch: the gateway firmware turns it on and off over the data line.\n5. Row wires follow the bus-board strip order 12V / B / A / GND. The gateway pair is A / B, like the Waveshare A+ / B-.\n6. JP1 = optional 120 ohm termination (solder bridge). Leave it open: the bus runs at 9600 baud over ~2 m. Never use both JP1\n    and the Waveshare 120R jumper, since both sit at the same end of the bus. SN65HVD72 receivers are failsafe, so no bias resistors.\n7. Strip BIN (backup data) is tied to DIN at J8 pin 3, as the strip maker specifies. Put the 1000 uF strip capacitor at the strip, not on this board.\n8. All wires solder straight into the board (plain solder pads): 16 AWG in the 1.5 mm2 pads (J10), 18 AWG in the 1 mm2 pads,\n    22-24 AWG in the 0.5 mm2 pads. SMD parts are JLCPCB-assembled (LCSC field); J1, C1 and the wires are hand-soldered."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 25.4 200.66 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.8 1.8)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
